# Full pages for the people on the themed trails who had only a short paragraph (Ifan, 12 November 2026:
# "a page on each of these"). long.py puts LONG into PEOPLE_LONG, so these replace the short text on person-<slug>.html.
# Format as in src/sixth_century.py: "## " starts a heading, "> words | who said it" is a quotation.
# Follow EDITORIAL.md: proud and true, nothing negative about the people of Welsh history, tradition in its own words.

LONG = {
 "gwenllian": (
  ["Gwenllian was a daughter of Gruffudd ap Cynan, king of Gwynedd, and of his queen Angharad. She was born on Ynys Môn around 1100, in the years when her father was winning his kingdom back from the Normans, and she grew up at the court of Aberffraw. Her brothers were Owain Gwynedd and Cadwaladr.",
   "## Princess of Deheubarth",
   "Around 1116 she married Gruffudd ap Rhys, the heir of Deheubarth, whose father Rhys ap Tewdwr had been the last king of the south. The Normans held most of his land, and for years the couple lived as outlaws in the hills and forests of Ystrad Tywi, striking at the castles and the settlers and slipping away again. They had several children. The youngest, Rhys, born in 1132, would become the Lord Rhys, the greatest ruler of the south.",
   "## The battle at Kidwelly, 1136",
   "When King Henry I died at the end of 1135, the Welsh rose across the country. Early in 1136 Gruffudd ap Rhys rode north to Gwynedd to bring her brothers into the war. While he was away, the Normans gathered their forces in the south, and Gwenllian gathered the men of Deheubarth and led them herself against Maurice de Londres, the Norman lord of Kidwelly. Gerald of Wales, writing fifty years later, says that she went into battle at the head of her army.",
   "She fought below Mynydd y Garreg, near Kidwelly Castle. The Normans had more men, and Gwenllian fell on the field. Her son Morgan was killed beside her and her son Maelgwn was taken prisoner. The field is still called Maes Gwenllian.",
   "## Revenge for Gwenllian",
   "Her death set Wales alight. Within months her brothers Owain and Cadwaladr came south with the army of Gwynedd, joined her husband, and routed the Normans at Crug Mawr near Cardigan. For generations afterwards, it is told, Welsh soldiers went into battle with the cry ‘Dial am Gwenllian!’, ‘Revenge for Gwenllian!’",
   "Few women of the Middle Ages led an army into battle, and Gwenllian did it for Wales. Her son Rhys grew up to win back the whole of Deheubarth, and Wales remembers her as a princess who gave her life for her country."],
  ["Merch Gruffudd ap Cynan, brenin Gwynedd, a’i frenhines Angharad oedd Gwenllian. Fe’i ganwyd ar Ynys Môn tua 1100, yn y blynyddoedd pan oedd ei thad yn ennill ei deyrnas yn ôl oddi ar y Normaniaid, a chafodd ei magu yn llys Aberffraw. Ei brodyr oedd Owain Gwynedd a Chadwaladr.",
   "## Tywysoges Deheubarth",
   "Tua 1116 priododd Gruffudd ap Rhys, etifedd Deheubarth, a’i dad Rhys ap Tewdwr oedd brenin olaf y de. Roedd y Normaniaid yn dal y rhan fwyaf o’i dir, ac am flynyddoedd bu’r ddau’n byw fel herwyr ym mryniau a fforestydd Ystrad Tywi, yn taro’r cestyll a’r gwladfawyr ac yn diflannu eto. Cawsant nifer o blant. Daeth yr ieuengaf, Rhys, a aned yn 1132, yn Arglwydd Rhys, rheolwr mwyaf y de.",
   "## Y frwydr yng Nghydweli, 1136",
   "Pan fu farw’r Brenin Harri I ar ddiwedd 1135, cododd y Cymry ar draws y wlad. Yn gynnar yn 1136 marchogodd Gruffudd ap Rhys i’r gogledd i dynnu ei brodyr hi i’r rhyfel. Tra oedd ef i ffwrdd, casglodd y Normaniaid eu lluoedd yn y de, a chasglodd Gwenllian wŷr Deheubarth a’u harwain ei hun yn erbyn Maurice de Londres, arglwydd Normanaidd Cydweli. Mae Gerallt Gymro, a ysgrifennai hanner can mlynedd wedyn, yn dweud iddi fynd i’r frwydr ar flaen ei byddin.",
   "Ymladdodd o dan Fynydd y Garreg, ger Castell Cydweli. Roedd gan y Normaniaid fwy o wŷr, a syrthiodd Gwenllian ar y maes. Lladdwyd ei mab Morgan wrth ei hochr a chymerwyd ei mab Maelgwn yn garcharor. Maes Gwenllian yw enw’r cae hyd heddiw.",
   "## Dial am Gwenllian",
   "Rhoddodd ei marwolaeth Gymru ar dân. O fewn misoedd daeth ei brodyr Owain a Chadwaladr i’r de gyda byddin Gwynedd, ymuno â’i gŵr, a chwalu’r Normaniaid yng Nghrug Mawr ger Aberteifi. Am genedlaethau wedyn, meddir, âi milwyr Cymru i’r frwydr dan weiddi ‘Dial am Gwenllian!’",
   "Prin oedd y menywod a arweiniodd fyddin i frwydr yn yr Oesoedd Canol, a gwnaeth Gwenllian hynny dros Gymru. Tyfodd ei mab Rhys i ennill Deheubarth gyfan yn ôl, ac mae Cymru’n ei chofio fel tywysoges a roddodd ei bywyd dros ei gwlad."]),

 "jemima-nicholas": (
  ["Jemima Nicholas was a shoemaker in Fishguard, in Pembrokeshire, at the end of the 18th century. She was born around 1750, she was famous in the town for her height and her strength, and she is remembered as Jemeima Fawr, Jemima the Great.",
   "## The last invasion of Britain",
   "On the evening of 22 February 1797 four French warships anchored off Carregwastad Point, near Fishguard, and about 1,400 soldiers of the Légion Noire came ashore under an American officer, Colonel William Tate. They had been meant for Bristol. It was the last time an enemy army landed on the soil of Britain.",
   "The people of Pembrokeshire turned out to meet them. The local Fencibles and Yeomanry gathered under Lord Cawdor, and farmers, quarrymen and townsfolk came with whatever they could carry. Within two days it was over: on 24 February the whole French force laid down its arms on Goodwick Sands.",
   "## Jemima and the pitchfork",
   "It is told that when Jemima heard the French had landed she took up a pitchfork, marched out to the fields at Llanwnda, and rounded up twelve French soldiers on her own. She brought them back to Fishguard and shut them up in St Mary’s Church until they could be handed over.",
   "Tradition also tells that the women of Fishguard, in their red flannel shawls and tall black hats, gathered on the hills above the bay, and that from a distance the French took them for a regiment of redcoats. Whatever the French saw, they did not fight on.",
   "## Remembered in Fishguard",
   "Jemima died in 1832, at the age of about 82, and was buried at St Mary’s Church. In 1897, a hundred years after the landing, a memorial stone was raised to her in the churchyard, calling her the Welsh heroine who boldly marched to meet the French invaders. For the two hundredth anniversary in 1997 the people of the town made the Last Invasion Tapestry, a hundred feet long, which shows her with her pitchfork, and the Pembroke Yeomanry still carries ‘Fishguard’ as a battle honour, the only one won on British soil."],
  ["Crydd yn Abergwaun, yn Sir Benfro, ar ddiwedd y ddeunawfed ganrif oedd Jemima Nicholas. Fe’i ganwyd tua 1750, roedd yn enwog yn y dref am ei thaldra a’i chryfder, ac fe’i cofir fel Jemeima Fawr.",
   "## Y glaniad olaf ym Mhrydain",
   "Ar noson 22 Chwefror 1797 angorodd pedair llong ryfel Ffrengig ger Pen Carregwastad, ger Abergwaun, a daeth tua 1,400 o filwyr y Légion Noire i’r lan dan swyddog Americanaidd, y Cyrnol William Tate. Bryste oedd eu nod. Dyna’r tro olaf i fyddin y gelyn lanio ar dir Prydain.",
   "Daeth pobl Sir Benfro allan i’w cyfarfod. Casglodd y milwyr lleol dan yr Arglwydd Cawdor, a daeth ffermwyr, chwarelwyr a phobl y dref â beth bynnag y gallent ei gario. O fewn deuddydd roedd y cyfan drosodd: ar 24 Chwefror gosododd y llu Ffrengig cyfan ei arfau i lawr ar Draeth Wdig.",
   "## Jemima a’r bicwarch",
   "Meddir, pan glywodd Jemima fod y Ffrancod wedi glanio, iddi gydio mewn picwarch, martsio allan i’r caeau yn Llanwnda, a dal deuddeg o filwyr Ffrengig ar ei phen ei hun. Daeth â nhw’n ôl i Abergwaun a’u cau yn Eglwys y Santes Fair nes y gellid eu trosglwyddo.",
   "Mae traddodiad hefyd yn dweud bod gwragedd Abergwaun, yn eu siolau gwlanen goch a’u hetiau duon tal, wedi casglu ar y bryniau uwchben y bae, ac i’r Ffrancod o bell eu cymryd am gatrawd o gotiau cochion. Beth bynnag a welodd y Ffrancod, ni wnaethant ymladd ymhellach.",
   "## Cofio yn Abergwaun",
   "Bu farw Jemima yn 1832, tua 82 oed, a’i chladdu yn Eglwys y Santes Fair. Yn 1897, gan mlynedd ar ôl y glaniad, codwyd carreg goffa iddi yn y fynwent, yn ei galw’n arwres Gymreig a aeth yn eofn i gyfarfod â’r goresgynwyr Ffrengig. I nodi’r daucanmlwyddiant yn 1997 gwnaeth pobl y dref Dapestri’r Goresgyniad Olaf, can troedfedd o hyd, sy’n ei dangos gyda’i phicwarch, ac mae Iwmyn Penfro’n dal i gario ‘Fishguard’ fel anrhydedd brwydr, yr unig un a enillwyd ar dir Prydain."]),

 "betsi-cadwaladr": (
  ["Elizabeth Cadwaladr was born on 24 May 1789 at Pen-rhiw, a farm near Bala in Meirionnydd. Her father Dafydd Cadwaladr was a Methodist preacher, and she grew up among the chapel people of Bala in the years of Thomas Charles. Her mother died when she was five.",
   "## A traveller of the world",
   "Betsi had a restless spirit. As a girl she left home, went into service in Liverpool, and later worked her way around the world as a servant on board ship, sailing to South America, the West Indies, Africa and Australia. She took the name Elizabeth Davis because the English found Cadwaladr hard to say, but she kept her Welsh all her life, and her Bible went with her everywhere.",
   "## Nursing in the Crimea",
   "Back in London she trained as a nurse at Guy’s Hospital. When the Crimean War broke out in 1854 and the newspapers told of soldiers dying for want of care, she volunteered at the age of sixty-five. She worked first at the great hospital at Scutari, where Florence Nightingale was in charge, and then, at her own wish, went on to Balaclava, close to the front line.",
   "There she ran the kitchen and the wards, fed the wounded with food she cooked herself, and nursed men straight from the trenches through the winter of 1854 to 1855. She was a woman who said what she thought, and she believed the sick should have what they needed at once, without waiting for forms and orders. She came home in 1855, worn out by the work.",
   "## Remembered",
   "Her memoirs, An Autobiography of Elizabeth Davis, a Balaclava Nurse, were written down with the help of Jane Williams of Ysgafell and published in 1857. She died in London in 1860 and lay for many years in an unmarked grave in Abney Park Cemetery, until a headstone was put up in 2012. The health board for north Wales, Betsi Cadwaladr University Health Board, carries her name, so that every day thousands of people are cared for under the name of the nurse from Bala."],
  ["Ganwyd Elizabeth Cadwaladr ar 24 Mai 1789 ym Mhen-rhiw, fferm ger y Bala ym Meirionnydd. Pregethwr Methodist oedd ei thad, Dafydd Cadwaladr, a chafodd ei magu ymhlith pobl capel y Bala yn nyddiau Thomas Charles. Bu farw ei mam pan oedd hi’n bump oed.",
   "## Teithiwr y byd",
   "Roedd ysbryd aflonydd yn Betsi. Yn ferch ifanc gadawodd ei chartref, aeth i weini yn Lerpwl, ac yn nes ymlaen gweithiodd ei ffordd o amgylch y byd fel morwyn ar fwrdd llong, gan hwylio i Dde America, India’r Gorllewin, Affrica ac Awstralia. Cymerodd yr enw Elizabeth Davis am fod y Saeson yn cael Cadwaladr yn anodd ei ddweud, ond cadwodd ei Chymraeg ar hyd ei hoes, ac aeth ei Beibl gyda hi i bobman.",
   "## Nyrsio yn y Crimea",
   "Yn ôl yn Llundain hyfforddodd fel nyrs yn Ysbyty Guy. Pan dorrodd Rhyfel y Crimea yn 1854 a’r papurau’n sôn am filwyr yn marw o eisiau gofal, gwirfoddolodd yn chwe deg pump oed. Gweithiodd yn gyntaf yn yr ysbyty mawr yn Scutari, lle’r oedd Florence Nightingale wrth y llyw, ac yna, o’i dewis ei hun, aeth ymlaen i Balaclava, yn agos at y rheng flaen.",
   "Yno rhedodd y gegin a’r wardiau, bwydo’r clwyfedig â bwyd a goginiodd ei hun, a nyrsio dynion yn syth o’r ffosydd drwy aeaf 1854 i 1855. Gwraig a ddywedai’r hyn a feddyliai oedd hi, a chredai y dylai’r cleifion gael yr hyn oedd ei angen arnynt ar unwaith, heb aros am ffurflenni a gorchmynion. Daeth adref yn 1855, wedi blino’n lân gan y gwaith.",
   "## Ei chofio",
   "Ysgrifennwyd ei hatgofion, An Autobiography of Elizabeth Davis, a Balaclava Nurse, gyda chymorth Jane Williams, Ysgafell, a’u cyhoeddi yn 1857. Bu farw yn Llundain yn 1860 a gorweddodd am flynyddoedd mewn bedd heb garreg ym Mynwent Abney Park, nes codi carreg fedd yn 2012. Mae bwrdd iechyd y gogledd, Bwrdd Iechyd Prifysgol Betsi Cadwaladr, yn dwyn ei henw, fel bod miloedd o bobl bob dydd yn cael gofal dan enw’r nyrs o’r Bala."]),

 "lady-llanover": (
  ["Augusta Waddington was born in 1802 at Tŷ Uchaf, Llanover, near Abergavenny in Gwent. Her family were not Welsh, but she grew up loving the language, the music and the customs of the people around her, and she gave her whole life to them. In 1823 she married Benjamin Hall, later Lord Llanover, and she is remembered as Lady Llanover.",
   "## Gwenynen Gwent",
   "She learnt Welsh and took the bardic name Gwenynen Gwent, the Bee of Gwent, and a busy bee she was. In 1834, at the eisteddfod held in Cardiff, she won the prize for an essay on the advantages of keeping the Welsh language and the national dress. She became the great patron of Cymreigyddion y Fenni, the Abergavenny Welsh society, whose eisteddfodau drew scholars and harpers from all over Wales, Brittany and beyond.",
   "## The triple harp and the Welsh costume",
   "Llanover Hall became a home for Welsh culture. She kept a household harpist, Thomas Gruffydd, encouraged the making of the Welsh triple harp and gave harps as prizes, and so helped to keep alive the instrument of the old Welsh harpers. Her servants spoke Welsh, the house was run in Welsh, and she supported Welsh services in the churches of the district.",
   "She painted watercolours of the dress of the women of Wales and urged people to wear Welsh flannel and tweed. The tall black hat, the shawl and the red cloak that we now call the Welsh national costume owe a great deal to her.",
   "## Saving the old books",
   "She helped the Welsh Manuscripts Society to print the treasures of Welsh literature, among them the Iolo Manuscripts in 1848, and she supported scholars who were making Welsh dictionaries. In 1867 she published a cookery book. She died in 1896, aged ninety-three, after seventy years of work for a language she had chosen as her own."],
  ["Ganwyd Augusta Waddington yn 1802 yn Nhŷ Uchaf, Llanofer, ger y Fenni yng Ngwent. Nid Cymry oedd ei theulu, ond tyfodd i garu iaith, cerddoriaeth ac arferion y bobl o’i chwmpas, a rhoddodd ei hoes gyfan iddynt. Yn 1823 priododd Benjamin Hall, yr Arglwydd Llanofer yn ddiweddarach, ac fe’i cofir fel Arglwyddes Llanofer.",
   "## Gwenynen Gwent",
   "Dysgodd Gymraeg a chymryd yr enw barddol Gwenynen Gwent, a gwenynen brysur oedd hi. Yn 1834, yn yr eisteddfod a gynhaliwyd yng Nghaerdydd, enillodd y wobr am draethawd ar fanteision cadw’r iaith Gymraeg a’r wisg genedlaethol. Daeth yn noddwr mawr Cymreigyddion y Fenni, a’u heisteddfodau’n denu ysgolheigion a thelynorion o bob rhan o Gymru, o Lydaw a thu hwnt.",
   "## Y delyn deires a’r wisg Gymreig",
   "Daeth Llys Llanofer yn gartref i ddiwylliant Cymru. Cadwai delynor teulu, Thomas Gruffydd, anogai wneud y delyn deires a rhoddai delynau’n wobrau, a thrwy hynny helpodd i gadw’n fyw offeryn yr hen delynorion Cymreig. Siaradai ei gweision Gymraeg, rhedid y tŷ yn Gymraeg, a chefnogai wasanaethau Cymraeg yn eglwysi’r ardal.",
   "Paentiodd ddyfrlliwiau o wisg gwragedd Cymru ac annog pobl i wisgo gwlanen a brethyn Cymreig. Mae’r het ddu dal, y siôl a’r clogyn coch a alwn heddiw yn wisg genedlaethol Cymru yn ddyledus iawn iddi.",
   "## Achub yr hen lyfrau",
   "Helpodd Gymdeithas Llawysgrifau Cymru i argraffu trysorau llenyddiaeth Gymraeg, yn eu plith Iolo Manuscripts yn 1848, a chefnogodd ysgolheigion a oedd yn llunio geiriaduron Cymraeg. Yn 1867 cyhoeddodd lyfr coginio. Bu farw yn 1896 yn naw deg tri oed, ar ôl saith deg mlynedd o waith dros iaith a ddewisodd yn iaith iddi hi ei hun."]),

 "gwen-john": (
  ["Gwen John was born in Haverfordwest on 22 June 1876 and grew up in Tenby, on the coast of Pembrokeshire, where she and her younger brother Augustus learnt to draw on the beach and the cliffs. Both went to the Slade School of Fine Art in London, where Gwen studied from 1895 to 1898 and won a prize for figure composition.",
   "## Paris",
   "In 1904 she went to Paris and stayed in France for the rest of her life. She earned her living at first by posing for other artists, among them the great sculptor Auguste Rodin, who made her the model for his monument to the painter Whistler. In her own small rooms she painted quietly and slowly, one picture at a time.",
   "From 1910 the American collector John Quinn bought her work and paid her a regular sum, which let her paint without worry. She moved to Meudon, outside Paris, and joined the Catholic Church in 1913. The sisters of the convent at Meudon asked her to paint their foundress, Mère Poussepin, and she made a whole series of these portraits.",
   "## A painter of stillness",
   "Her pictures are mostly of women sitting alone, of cats, of a corner of her room with a chair and a window, painted in soft greys, browns and blues. They are small and very still, and they have a feeling of deep calm. She showed her work in a single exhibition of her own, in London in 1926. She died in Dieppe in 1939.",
   "> In fifty years’ time I will be known as the brother of Gwen John. | Augustus John",
   "Her brother was right. Today Gwen John is counted among the finest British artists of the twentieth century. The National Museum in Cardiff holds a large collection of her paintings and drawings, and her self-portrait hangs in the Tate."],
  ["Ganwyd Gwen John yn Hwlffordd ar 22 Mehefin 1876 a’i magu yn Ninbych-y-pysgod, ar arfordir Sir Benfro, lle dysgodd hi a’i brawd iau Augustus dynnu llun ar y traeth a’r clogwyni. Aeth y ddau i Ysgol Gelf y Slade yn Llundain, lle bu Gwen yn astudio o 1895 i 1898 ac ennill gwobr am gyfansoddi ffigurau.",
   "## Paris",
   "Yn 1904 aeth i Baris ac aros yn Ffrainc am weddill ei hoes. Enillai ei bywoliaeth ar y dechrau drwy fodelu i arlunwyr eraill, yn eu plith y cerflunydd mawr Auguste Rodin, a’i gwnaeth yn fodel ar gyfer ei gofeb i’r arlunydd Whistler. Yn ei hystafelloedd bach ei hun paentiai’n dawel ac yn araf, un llun ar y tro.",
   "O 1910 prynai’r casglwr Americanaidd John Quinn ei gwaith a thalu swm cyson iddi, a’i galluogodd i baentio heb bryder. Symudodd i Meudon, y tu allan i Baris, ac ymunodd â’r Eglwys Gatholig yn 1913. Gofynnodd chwiorydd lleiandy Meudon iddi baentio eu sylfaenydd, Mère Poussepin, a gwnaeth gyfres gyfan o’r portreadau hyn.",
   "## Arlunydd y llonyddwch",
   "Lluniau o fenywod yn eistedd ar eu pen eu hunain yw’r rhan fwyaf o’i gwaith, a chathod, a chornel o’i hystafell gyda chadair a ffenestr, mewn llwydion, brown a gleision meddal. Maent yn fach ac yn llonydd iawn, ac mae ynddynt dawelwch dwfn. Dangosodd ei gwaith mewn un arddangosfa o’i heiddo ei hun, yn Llundain yn 1926. Bu farw yn Dieppe yn 1939.",
   "> Ymhen hanner can mlynedd fe’m cofir fel brawd Gwen John. | Augustus John",
   "Roedd ei brawd yn iawn. Heddiw cyfrifir Gwen John ymhlith arlunwyr gorau Prydain yn yr ugeinfed ganrif. Mae gan yr Amgueddfa Genedlaethol yng Nghaerdydd gasgliad mawr o’i phaentiadau a’i lluniadau, ac mae ei hunanbortread yn y Tate."]),

 "megan-lloyd-george": (
  ["Megan Lloyd George was born in Criccieth on 22 April 1902, the youngest child of David Lloyd George and his wife Margaret. Welsh was the language of her home. She was a schoolgirl at 10 and 11 Downing Street while her father led Britain through the First World War, and she grew up surrounded by politics.",
   "## The first woman MP for Wales",
   "In 1929, at the age of twenty-seven, she was elected Liberal Member of Parliament for Anglesey. She was the first woman ever to sit for a Welsh constituency. She held the seat for twenty-two years, speaking for the farmers, the fishermen and the quarrymen of Môn, for better housing and for the rights of women.",
   "## A Parliament for Wales",
   "Megan believed that Wales should govern itself. In the 1950s she was president of the Parliament for Wales Campaign, which crossed party lines and gathered a petition of about 250,000 signatures. It was handed to Parliament in 1956. The Senedd that opened in 1999 was the fruit of campaigns like hers.",
   "In 1955 she joined the Labour Party, and in 1957 she won the Carmarthen by-election. She represented Carmarthen until her death.",
   "## Her legacy",
   "Megan Lloyd George died at Criccieth on 14 May 1966 and was buried there near her father. Shortly before her death she was made a Companion of Honour. The by-election that followed her death, in July 1966, was won by Gwynfor Evans, the first member of Plaid Cymru ever to sit in Parliament. She had opened doors for the women of Wales in politics, and for the idea of a Welsh parliament."],
  ["Ganwyd Megan Lloyd George yng Nghricieth ar 22 Ebrill 1902, plentyn ieuengaf David Lloyd George a’i wraig Margaret. Cymraeg oedd iaith ei chartref. Roedd yn ferch ysgol yn 10 ac 11 Downing Street tra oedd ei thad yn arwain Prydain drwy’r Rhyfel Byd Cyntaf, a chafodd ei magu yng nghanol gwleidyddiaeth.",
   "## Yr AS benywaidd gyntaf dros Gymru",
   "Yn 1929, yn saith ar hugain oed, fe’i hetholwyd yn Aelod Seneddol Rhyddfrydol dros Ynys Môn. Hi oedd y fenyw gyntaf erioed i gynrychioli etholaeth yng Nghymru. Daliodd y sedd am ddwy flynedd ar hugain, gan siarad dros ffermwyr, pysgotwyr a chwarelwyr Môn, dros well tai a thros hawliau menywod.",
   "## Senedd i Gymru",
   "Credai Megan y dylai Cymru ei llywodraethu ei hun. Yn y 1950au hi oedd llywydd Ymgyrch Senedd i Gymru, a groesodd ffiniau’r pleidiau a chasglu deiseb o tua 250,000 o enwau. Fe’i cyflwynwyd i’r Senedd yn Llundain yn 1956. Ffrwyth ymgyrchoedd fel ei hymgyrch hi oedd y Senedd a agorodd yn 1999.",
   "Yn 1955 ymunodd â’r Blaid Lafur, ac yn 1957 enillodd isetholiad Caerfyrddin. Bu’n cynrychioli Caerfyrddin hyd ei marwolaeth.",
   "## Ei hetifeddiaeth",
   "Bu farw Megan Lloyd George yng Nghricieth ar 14 Mai 1966 a’i chladdu yno ger ei thad. Ychydig cyn ei marwolaeth fe’i gwnaed yn Gydymaith Anrhydedd. Enillwyd yr isetholiad a ddilynodd ei marwolaeth, ym mis Gorffennaf 1966, gan Gwynfor Evans, aelod cyntaf Plaid Cymru erioed i eistedd yn San Steffan. Agorodd ddrysau i fenywod Cymru mewn gwleidyddiaeth, ac i’r syniad o senedd i Gymru."]),

 "taliesin": (
  ["Taliesin is the earliest poet of the Welsh language whose own words we still have. He sang in the second half of the 6th century, in the Old North, the British kingdoms that then stretched from Wales up through what is now northern England and southern Scotland, where the people spoke the language that became Welsh.",
   "## The poet of Urien",
   "His great patron was Urien, king of Rheged, the bravest and most generous ruler of his day. Taliesin praised him as a lion in battle and a giver of gold, horses and mead, and he mourned Urien’s son Owain when he fell. He also sang for Cynan Garwyn, king of Powys, and for Gwallawg, king of Elfed. The History of the Britons, written in Wales in 829, names Taliesin among the poets who were famous in that age, with Aneirin.",
   "In the poems’ own refrain, the poet says that until he grows old and dies, he will never be glad unless he praises Urien.",
   "## The Book of Taliesin",
   "His poems were handed down by the bards for seven hundred years and copied into the Book of Taliesin around 1350, which is now in the National Library of Wales. The great scholar Sir Ifor Williams showed that a dozen of its poems are the work of the 6th-century poet himself, which makes them among the oldest poems in any living language of Europe.",
   "## Taliesin of the tales",
   "Later Wales made him a figure of wonder. The tale of Taliesin tells that he was first the boy Gwion Bach, who tasted three drops from the cauldron of Ceridwen beside Llyn Tegid and gained all knowledge, was swallowed by her and reborn, and was found as a baby in a fishing weir by Elffin. At the court of Maelgwn Gwynedd he outsang all the king’s bards. For the poets who came after, Taliesin was the model of the inspired bard, and a grave near Tal-y-bont in Ceredigion is still called Bedd Taliesin."],
  ["Taliesin yw’r bardd Cymraeg cynharaf y mae ei eiriau ei hun gennym o hyd. Canai yn ail hanner y chweched ganrif, yn yr Hen Ogledd, y teyrnasoedd Brythonig a ymestynnai bryd hynny o Gymru drwy’r hyn sydd heddiw’n ogledd Lloegr a de’r Alban, lle siaradai’r bobl yr iaith a ddaeth yn Gymraeg.",
   "## Bardd Urien",
   "Ei noddwr mawr oedd Urien, brenin Rheged, rheolwr dewraf a haelaf ei oes. Canodd Taliesin ei glod fel llew mewn brwydr a rhoddwr aur, meirch a medd, a galarodd am Owain fab Urien pan syrthiodd. Canodd hefyd i Gynan Garwyn, brenin Powys, ac i Wallawg, brenin Elfed. Mae Historia Brittonum, a ysgrifennwyd yng Nghymru yn 829, yn enwi Taliesin ymhlith y beirdd a oedd yn enwog yn yr oes honno, gydag Aneirin.",
   "Yn nhrawsgyrch y cerddi eu hunain, dywed y bardd na fydd byth yn llawen, nes iddo heneiddio a marw, oni bai ei fod yn moli Urien.",
   "## Llyfr Taliesin",
   "Trosglwyddwyd ei gerddi gan y beirdd am saith can mlynedd a’u copïo i Lyfr Taliesin tua 1350, sydd heddiw yn Llyfrgell Genedlaethol Cymru. Dangosodd yr ysgolhaig mawr Syr Ifor Williams fod dwsin o’i gerddi’n waith y bardd o’r chweched ganrif ei hun, sy’n eu gwneud ymhlith y cerddi hynaf yn unrhyw un o ieithoedd byw Ewrop.",
   "## Taliesin y chwedlau",
   "Gwnaeth Cymru ddiweddarach ef yn ffigwr rhyfeddol. Mae Hanes Taliesin yn adrodd mai’r bachgen Gwion Bach oedd ef gyntaf, a brofodd dri diferyn o bair Ceridwen ger Llyn Tegid ac ennill pob gwybodaeth, a’i llyncodd hi a’i aileni, a chafodd ei ganfod yn faban mewn cored bysgod gan Elffin. Yn llys Maelgwn Gwynedd trechodd holl feirdd y brenin. I’r beirdd a ddaeth ar ei ôl, Taliesin oedd delfryd y bardd awenus, a gelwir bedd ger Tal-y-bont yng Ngheredigion yn Fedd Taliesin hyd heddiw."]),

 "dafydd-ap-gwilym": (
  ["Dafydd ap Gwilym is the greatest poet of medieval Wales, and one of the great poets of medieval Europe. He was born at Brogynin, near Penrhyn-coch in northern Ceredigion, around 1315 to 1320, into a family of gentry who served as officials in the south. He wrote between about 1340 and 1370.",
   "## Learning the craft",
   "He learnt his art from his uncle, Llywelyn ap Gwilym, constable of Newcastle Emlyn, a man of learning and a patron of poets. His own great patron was Ifor Hael, Ifor the Generous, of Basaleg in Gwent, whose open-handed hall he praised in some of his finest poems.",
   "## The cywydd",
   "Dafydd took the cywydd, a short metre in rhyming couplets bound by cynghanedd, the Welsh music of sounds, and made it the great form of Welsh poetry for the next three hundred years. Where the court poets before him had praised princes in grand and difficult verse, he wrote in a lively, natural voice about love, the woods and the weather, and about himself.",
   "His poems are full of joy and wit. He sends a seagull, a wind or a skylark as a messenger to the girl he loves. He meets his love in a birch grove. He is lost in the mist on his way to her. He tells against himself the story of a night in an inn, where in the dark he knocks over a table and wakes three Englishmen. Two women fill his love poems: Morfudd, golden-haired, and Dyddgu, dark-haired.",
   "## Strata Florida",
   "About 150 poems are thought to be his. Tradition holds that he was buried at the abbey of Strata Florida, under a yew tree, and a memorial stone marks the place today. Six hundred years later his poems are still read, learnt and loved in Wales."],
  ["Dafydd ap Gwilym yw bardd mwyaf Cymru’r Oesoedd Canol, ac un o feirdd mawr Ewrop yr Oesoedd Canol. Fe’i ganwyd ym Mrogynin, ger Penrhyn-coch yng ngogledd Ceredigion, rywbryd tua 1315 i 1320, i deulu o uchelwyr a wasanaethai fel swyddogion yn y de. Canai rhwng tua 1340 a 1370.",
   "## Dysgu’r grefft",
   "Dysgodd ei grefft gan ei ewythr, Llywelyn ap Gwilym, cwnstabl Castellnewydd Emlyn, gŵr dysgedig a noddwr beirdd. Ei noddwr mawr ei hun oedd Ifor Hael o Fasaleg yng Ngwent, a chanodd glod ei neuadd agored mewn rhai o’i gerddi gorau.",
   "## Y cywydd",
   "Cymerodd Dafydd y cywydd, mesur byr mewn cwpledi sy’n odli ac wedi’u rhwymo gan gynghanedd, cerddoriaeth seiniau’r Gymraeg, a’i wneud yn brif fesur barddoniaeth Gymraeg am y tri chan mlynedd nesaf. Lle bu beirdd y llys o’i flaen yn moli tywysogion mewn cerddi mawreddog ac anodd, canodd ef mewn llais bywiog a naturiol am serch, y coed a’r tywydd, ac amdano’i hun.",
   "Mae ei gerddi’n llawn llawenydd a ffraethineb. Mae’n anfon gwylan, y gwynt neu’r ehedydd yn llatai at y ferch a gâr. Mae’n cyfarfod â’i gariad mewn llwyn bedw. Mae’n mynd ar goll yn y niwl ar ei ffordd ati. Mae’n adrodd yn ei erbyn ei hun hanes noson mewn tafarn, lle mae’n taro bwrdd drosodd yn y tywyllwch ac yn deffro tri Sais. Mae dwy ferch yn llenwi ei gerddi serch: Morfudd, y ferch felyn ei gwallt, a Dyddgu, y ferch bryd tywyll.",
   "## Ystrad Fflur",
   "Credir bod tua 150 o gerddi yn eiddo iddo. Yn ôl traddodiad fe’i claddwyd yn abaty Ystrad Fflur, dan ywen, ac mae carreg goffa’n nodi’r fan heddiw. Chwe chan mlynedd yn ddiweddarach mae ei gerddi’n dal i gael eu darllen, eu dysgu a’u caru yng Nghymru."]),

 "william-morgan": (
  ["William Morgan gave Wales the whole Bible in its own language. He was born in 1545 at Tŷ Mawr, a farmhouse in the valley of the Wybrnant near Penmachno, in Gwynedd. The house still stands and is cared for by the National Trust.",
   "## A scholar from the hills",
   "He went to St John’s College, Cambridge, where he studied for many years and learnt Hebrew and Greek, the languages in which the Bible was first written. He became a priest, and in 1578 vicar of Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant, on the border of Powys, where most of his great work was done.",
   "## The Welsh Bible of 1588",
   "In 1563 Parliament had ordered that the Bible and the Prayer Book be put into Welsh, and in 1567 William Salesbury and Bishop Richard Davies published the New Testament. But the Old Testament, the greater part, was still not in Welsh. William Morgan took on the whole task. He translated the Old Testament from the Hebrew, revised the New Testament, and in 1588 the complete Bible in Welsh was printed in London. A copy was placed in every parish church in Wales.",
   "His Welsh was the rich, dignified language of the poets, clear enough for every congregation to understand. For more than three centuries his Bible was the book that every Welsh family knew, read aloud at home, in church and later in chapel and Sunday school. It gave the Welsh a standard language for reading and writing at the very time when other languages of Britain were fading, and many believe it saved Welsh.",
   "## Bishop",
   "He was made Bishop of Llandaff in 1595 and Bishop of St Asaph in 1601. He died on 10 September 1604. A memorial to the translators of the Welsh Bible stands outside St Asaph Cathedral, with William Morgan at its centre."],
  ["William Morgan a roddodd y Beibl cyfan i Gymru yn ei hiaith ei hun. Fe’i ganwyd yn 1545 yn Nhŷ Mawr, ffermdy yng nghwm Wybrnant ger Penmachno, yng Ngwynedd. Mae’r tŷ yn dal i sefyll ac yng ngofal yr Ymddiriedolaeth Genedlaethol.",
   "## Ysgolhaig o’r bryniau",
   "Aeth i Goleg Sant Ioan, Caergrawnt, lle bu’n astudio am flynyddoedd lawer a dysgu Hebraeg a Groeg, yr ieithoedd yr ysgrifennwyd y Beibl ynddynt gyntaf. Daeth yn offeiriad, ac yn 1578 yn ficer Llanrhaeadr-ym-Mochnant, ar ffin Powys, lle gwnaed y rhan fwyaf o’i waith mawr.",
   "## Beibl Cymraeg 1588",
   "Yn 1563 roedd y Senedd wedi gorchymyn rhoi’r Beibl a’r Llyfr Gweddi yn Gymraeg, ac yn 1567 cyhoeddodd William Salesbury a’r Esgob Richard Davies y Testament Newydd. Ond nid oedd yr Hen Destament, y rhan fwyaf, yn Gymraeg o hyd. Ymgymerodd William Morgan â’r dasg gyfan. Cyfieithodd yr Hen Destament o’r Hebraeg, diwygio’r Testament Newydd, ac yn 1588 argraffwyd y Beibl cyflawn yn Gymraeg yn Llundain. Rhoddwyd copi ym mhob eglwys blwyf yng Nghymru.",
   "Ei Gymraeg oedd iaith gyfoethog, urddasol y beirdd, yn ddigon clir i bob cynulleidfa ei deall. Am fwy na thair canrif ei Feibl ef oedd y llyfr a adwaenai pob teulu yng Nghymru, a ddarllenid ar goedd ar yr aelwyd, yn yr eglwys ac yn ddiweddarach yn y capel a’r Ysgol Sul. Rhoddodd i’r Cymry iaith safonol i ddarllen ac ysgrifennu ar yr union adeg pan oedd ieithoedd eraill Prydain yn pylu, a chred llawer mai ef a achubodd y Gymraeg.",
   "## Esgob",
   "Fe’i gwnaed yn Esgob Llandaf yn 1595 ac yn Esgob Llanelwy yn 1601. Bu farw ar 10 Medi 1604. Mae cofeb i gyfieithwyr y Beibl Cymraeg yn sefyll y tu allan i Eglwys Gadeiriol Llanelwy, a William Morgan yn ei chanol."]),

 "pantycelyn": (
  ["William Williams, known after his home as Pantycelyn, is the greatest hymn writer of Wales. He was born in 1717 at Cefn-coed, in the parish of Llanfair-ar-y-bryn near Llandovery, in Carmarthenshire. His family were Dissenters, and he was meant to become a doctor.",
   "## The call",
   "Around 1737, as a young man, he heard Howell Harris preaching in the churchyard at Talgarth, and his life changed. He became a deacon of the Church of England and a curate in Llanwrtyd, but his Methodist preaching meant the bishop would not ordain him priest, and from 1743 he gave himself wholly to the Methodist revival, alongside Howell Harris and Daniel Rowland.",
   "For half a century he travelled the roads of Wales on horseback, preaching, teaching and caring for the seiadau, the small Methodist societies. It is said that he rode some 150,000 miles. He married Mary Francis, and they made their home at Pantycelyn, the farm that gave him his name.",
   "## The sweet singer",
   "Wales calls him Y Pêr Ganiedydd, the Sweet Singer. He wrote about 900 hymns in Welsh, beginning with the collection Aleluia in 1744, and more in English. His hymns are full of longing, of the journey of the soul through the wilderness to the promised land, and they were sung by whole congregations in the great revivals.",
   "> Arglwydd, arwain trwy’r anialwch / fi, bererin gwael ei wedd. | William Williams Pantycelyn, 1745",
   "In English this hymn became ‘Guide me, O thou great Jehovah’. Sung to the tune Cwm Rhondda, written by John Hughes in 1907, it is heard wherever the Welsh gather, from chapels to rugby grounds. He also wrote long poems, among them Theomemphus, and books of guidance for the societies. He died in 1791 and is buried at Llanfair-ar-y-bryn."],
  ["William Williams, a adwaenir wrth enw ei gartref fel Pantycelyn, yw emynydd mwyaf Cymru. Fe’i ganwyd yn 1717 yng Nghefn-coed, ym mhlwyf Llanfair-ar-y-bryn ger Llanymddyfri, yn Sir Gaerfyrddin. Anghydffurfwyr oedd ei deulu, a’r bwriad oedd iddo fod yn feddyg.",
   "## Yr alwad",
   "Tua 1737, yn ŵr ifanc, clywodd Howell Harris yn pregethu ym mynwent Talgarth, a newidiodd ei fywyd. Daeth yn ddiacon yn Eglwys Loegr ac yn gurad yn Llanwrtyd, ond am ei fod yn pregethu fel Methodist ni fynnai’r esgob ei ordeinio’n offeiriad, ac o 1743 rhoddodd ei hun yn llwyr i’r diwygiad Methodistaidd, gyda Howell Harris a Daniel Rowland.",
   "Am hanner canrif teithiodd ffyrdd Cymru ar gefn ceffyl, yn pregethu, yn dysgu ac yn gofalu am y seiadau. Dywedir iddo farchogaeth tua 150,000 o filltiroedd. Priododd Mary Francis, a gwnaethant eu cartref ym Mhantycelyn, y fferm a roddodd iddo ei enw.",
   "## Y Pêr Ganiedydd",
   "Mae Cymru’n ei alw’n Bêr Ganiedydd. Ysgrifennodd tua 900 o emynau Cymraeg, gan ddechrau gyda’r casgliad Aleluia yn 1744, a rhagor yn Saesneg. Mae ei emynau’n llawn hiraeth, am daith yr enaid drwy’r anialwch i’r wlad addawedig, ac fe’u canwyd gan gynulleidfaoedd cyfan yn y diwygiadau mawr.",
   "> Arglwydd, arwain trwy’r anialwch / fi, bererin gwael ei wedd. | William Williams Pantycelyn, 1745",
   "Yn Saesneg daeth yr emyn hwn yn ‘Guide me, O thou great Jehovah’. Wedi’i chanu ar y dôn Cwm Rhondda, a gyfansoddodd John Hughes yn 1907, fe’i clywir lle bynnag y daw’r Cymry ynghyd, o’r capeli i’r meysydd rygbi. Ysgrifennodd hefyd gerddi hir, yn eu plith Theomemphus, a llyfrau o gyfarwyddyd i’r seiadau. Bu farw yn 1791 ac fe’i claddwyd yn Llanfair-ar-y-bryn."]),

 "dic-penderyn": (
  ["Dic Penderyn was the name by which Wales remembers Richard Lewis, a young miner hanged for the Merthyr Rising of 1831. He was born in 1808 at Aberafan, and as a young man he moved to Merthyr Tydfil, then the greatest iron town in the world, to work in the mines that fed the furnaces.",
   "## The Merthyr Rising",
   "In 1831 times were hard in Merthyr. Wages were cut, and the Court of Requests seized the goods of working families for their debts. In the first days of June the workers rose. They took back the goods that had been seized, raised a red flag, which many believe was the first time it was flown as a banner of the working people in Britain, and for some days they held the town.",
   "On 3 June soldiers of the 93rd Highlanders stood outside the Castle Inn, where the ironmasters and magistrates had gathered. The crowd pressed forward, the soldiers opened fire, and many of the people of Merthyr were killed. In the struggle a soldier, Private Donald Black, was wounded in the leg.",
   "## The trial",
   "Dic Penderyn and Lewis Lewis, Lewsyn yr Heliwr, were among those arrested. Dic was charged with wounding Donald Black. He said to the end that he had not done it, and the evidence against him was thin, but he was found guilty and sentenced to death. More than 11,000 people signed a petition asking for his life to be spared. It was refused.",
   "> O Arglwydd, dyma gamwedd. | Dic Penderyn on the scaffold, 13 August 1831",
   "He was hanged at Cardiff on 13 August 1831, aged twenty-three. His words were ‘O Lord, here is injustice’. His body was carried home over the hills to Aberafan by a great crowd, and he was buried at St Mary’s Church.",
   "## The first martyr of the Welsh working class",
   "Years later, in 1874, a man named Ieuan Parker is said to have confessed in America that it was he who had wounded the soldier. Dic Penderyn has been remembered ever since as the first martyr of the working people of Wales. A plaque marks the place of his hanging at Cardiff Market, and his story is told in songs, novels and plays."],
  ["Dic Penderyn yw’r enw y mae Cymru’n cofio Richard Lewis wrtho, glöwr ifanc a grogwyd am Wrthryfel Merthyr yn 1831. Fe’i ganwyd yn 1808 yn Aberafan, ac yn ŵr ifanc symudodd i Ferthyr Tudful, tref haearn fwyaf y byd bryd hynny, i weithio yn y pyllau a fwydai’r ffwrneisi.",
   "## Gwrthryfel Merthyr",
   "Yn 1831 roedd hi’n galed ym Merthyr. Torrwyd cyflogau, ac roedd Llys y Ceisiadau’n cymryd eiddo teuluoedd y gweithwyr am eu dyledion. Yn nyddiau cyntaf Mehefin cododd y gweithwyr. Cymerasant yn ôl yr eiddo a atafaelwyd, codi baner goch, y tro cyntaf, yn ôl llawer, iddi gael ei chodi fel baner y werin bobl ym Mhrydain, ac am rai dyddiau daliasant y dref.",
   "Ar 3 Mehefin safodd milwyr y 93rd Highlanders y tu allan i Westy’r Castell, lle’r oedd y meistri haearn a’r ynadon wedi ymgasglu. Gwthiodd y dyrfa ymlaen, taniodd y milwyr, a lladdwyd llawer o bobl Merthyr. Yn yr ymrafael clwyfwyd milwr, Donald Black, yn ei goes.",
   "## Yr achos llys",
   "Roedd Dic Penderyn a Lewis Lewis, Lewsyn yr Heliwr, ymhlith y rhai a arestiwyd. Cyhuddwyd Dic o glwyfo Donald Black. Dywedodd hyd y diwedd nad ef a’i gwnaeth, ac roedd y dystiolaeth yn ei erbyn yn denau, ond fe’i cafwyd yn euog a’i ddedfrydu i farwolaeth. Arwyddodd mwy na 11,000 o bobl ddeiseb yn gofyn am arbed ei fywyd. Fe’i gwrthodwyd.",
   "> O Arglwydd, dyma gamwedd. | Dic Penderyn ar y crocbren, 13 Awst 1831",
   "Fe’i crogwyd yng Nghaerdydd ar 13 Awst 1831, yn dair ar hugain oed. Cariwyd ei gorff adref dros y bryniau i Aberafan gan dyrfa fawr, ac fe’i claddwyd yn Eglwys y Santes Fair.",
   "## Merthyr cyntaf y dosbarth gweithiol",
   "Flynyddoedd wedyn, yn 1874, dywedir i ddyn o’r enw Ieuan Parker gyfaddef yn America mai ef a glwyfodd y milwr. Mae Dic Penderyn wedi’i gofio byth ers hynny fel merthyr cyntaf gweithwyr Cymru. Mae plac yn nodi man ei grogi ym Marchnad Caerdydd, ac mae ei hanes yn cael ei adrodd mewn caneuon, nofelau a dramâu."]),

 "aneurin-bevan": (
  ["Aneurin Bevan, Nye to everyone who knew him, was born in Tredegar, in Monmouthshire, on 15 November 1897, the son of a coal miner, David Bevan. He left school at thirteen and went to work underground at Ty-trist colliery.",
   "## Tredegar",
   "He read everything he could find in the library of the Tredegar Workmen’s Institute, and he fought a stammer to become one of the great speakers of his age. The miners sent him to the Central Labour College in London in 1919, and he came back to serve his town as a union leader and councillor.",
   "Tredegar had something rare: the Tredegar Medical Aid Society, through which the workers paid a few pence a week so that every family could see a doctor and get medicine free when they needed it. Nye never forgot it. Later he said he wanted to ‘Tredegarise’ Britain.",
   "## The NHS",
   "In 1929 he was elected Member of Parliament for Ebbw Vale, which he served until his death. In 1945 he became Minister of Health, and against strong opposition he created the National Health Service. On 5 July 1948 it opened its doors: for the first time, everyone in Britain could see a doctor, go to hospital and get treatment, free at the point of need. He also led the building of hundreds of thousands of new council homes after the war.",
   "> The NHS will last as long as there are folk left with faith to fight for it. | Aneurin Bevan",
   "## His legacy",
   "Nye Bevan died on 6 July 1960 and was buried near Tredegar. Memorial stones stand on the hill above the town, where he used to speak to the people of the valleys. In 2004 the people of Wales voted him the greatest Welsh hero of all. The health service he founded has cared for every family in Britain for more than seventy-five years."],
  ["Ganwyd Aneurin Bevan, Nye i bawb a’i hadwaenai, yn Nhredegar, yn Sir Fynwy, ar 15 Tachwedd 1897, mab i löwr, David Bevan. Gadawodd yr ysgol yn dair ar ddeg a mynd i weithio dan ddaear ym mhwll Ty-trist.",
   "## Tredegar",
   "Darllenai bopeth y gallai ddod o hyd iddo yn llyfrgell Neuadd y Gweithwyr Tredegar, ac ymladdodd yn erbyn atal dweud i ddod yn un o areithwyr mawr ei oes. Anfonodd y glowyr ef i’r Coleg Llafur Canolog yn Llundain yn 1919, a daeth yn ôl i wasanaethu ei dref fel arweinydd undeb a chynghorydd.",
   "Roedd gan Dredegar rywbeth prin: Cymdeithas Cymorth Meddygol Tredegar, lle talai’r gweithwyr ychydig geiniogau’r wythnos fel y gallai pob teulu weld meddyg a chael moddion am ddim pan oedd angen. Nid anghofiodd Nye hynny erioed. Yn ddiweddarach dywedodd ei fod am ‘Dredegareiddio’ Prydain.",
   "## Y Gwasanaeth Iechyd",
   "Yn 1929 fe’i hetholwyd yn Aelod Seneddol dros Lynebwy, a bu’n ei gwasanaethu hyd ei farw. Yn 1945 daeth yn Weinidog Iechyd, ac yn erbyn gwrthwynebiad cryf creodd y Gwasanaeth Iechyd Gwladol. Ar 5 Gorffennaf 1948 agorodd ei ddrysau: am y tro cyntaf, gallai pawb ym Mhrydain weld meddyg, mynd i’r ysbyty a chael triniaeth, am ddim pan oedd angen. Arweiniodd hefyd y gwaith o godi cannoedd o filoedd o dai cyngor newydd ar ôl y rhyfel.",
   "> Bydd y Gwasanaeth Iechyd yn para cyhyd ag y bydd pobl ar ôl â’r ffydd i ymladd drosto. | Aneurin Bevan",
   "## Ei etifeddiaeth",
   "Bu farw Nye Bevan ar 6 Gorffennaf 1960 a’i gladdu ger Tredegar. Mae meini coffa’n sefyll ar y bryn uwchben y dref, lle byddai’n annerch pobl y cymoedd. Yn 2004 pleidleisiodd pobl Cymru mai ef oedd yr arwr Cymreig mwyaf oll. Mae’r gwasanaeth iechyd a sefydlodd wedi gofalu am bob teulu ym Mhrydain am fwy na saith deg pum mlynedd."]),
}
